🧬 HBB Gene & E6V Mutation – Research Summary

Prepared by: Zahraa Qasim
Year: 2025

🔹 Introduction

The HBB gene encodes the beta chain of hemoglobin.
It plays a central role in oxygen transport.
Mutations in this gene cause inherited blood disorders, the most known being Sickle Cell Disease (SCD).

🔹 HBB Gene Location

Located on chromosome 11.

🔹 Literature Review

The HBB gene contains three exons.

Produces a protein of 147 amino acids.

Highly sensitive to mutations.

Associated with Beta-thalassemia and Sickle Cell Disease.

🔹 BLAST & UniProt Analysis

BLASTp shows 95–100% similarity with primates.

HBB is evolutionarily conserved.

UniProt identifies heme-binding regions and protein stability zones.

🔹 E6V Mutation (rs334)

Missense mutation.

Nucleotide substitution: GAG → GTG.

Amino acid change: Glutamic acid → Valine at position 6.

ClinVar: Pathogenic.

SIFT & PolyPhen: Damaging.

🔹 Multiple Sequence Alignment

Position 6 is highly conserved across mammals.

Any mutation here has a strong structural and functional impact.

🔹 Discussion

A single amino acid substitution can lead to full disease.

The mutation causes hemoglobin polymerization, producing the sickle shape.

Bioinformatics tools allow predicting and understanding these effects.

🔹 Conclusion

HBB is a well-studied genetic disease model.

E6V mutation clearly explains the molecular mechanism of SCD.

Bioinformatics accelerates genetic research and improves clinical predictions.

